\title{CompPoly}
\taxon{Tool}
\author{dhsorens}
\meta{toc}{true}
\date{2026-08-07}

\p{
    [CompPoly](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/CompPoly) is a formally verified [Lean](dhsorens-lean) library for computable polynomial operations over rings and finite fields.
    I maintain it as part of [Protocol Snarkification](dhsorens-0020) at the [Ethereum Foundation](dhsorens-ef), where it is meant to serve as the computable core underneath [ArkLib](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib).
}

\p{
    The point of CompPoly is not just to have polynomials in Lean, but to have representations that you can actually \em{run}.
    Mathlib's polynomial types are excellent for abstract algebra, but they are not designed as an executable engine for zero-knowledge verification work.
    CompPoly provides computable univariate, multivariate, multilinear, and bivariate polynomials, with proved equivalences back to the Mathlib counterparts where that bridge matters.
    I have written more about why [computable specifications](dhsorens-0025) matter in this setting.
}

\p{
    In broad strokes, the library covers:
    \ul{
        \li{\em{Univariate} (\code{CPolynomial}): coefficient-sequence polynomials with evaluation, interpolation (including Lagrange), and a ring equivalence to Mathlib \code{Polynomial}.}
        \li{\em{Multivariate} (\code{CMvPolynomial}): sparse monomial maps with a full core API and equivalence to Mathlib \code{MvPolynomial}.}
        \li{\em{Multilinear} (\code{CMlPolynomial} / \code{CMlPolynomialEval}): coefficient and Boolean-hypercube evaluation forms.}
        \li{\em{Bivariate} (\code{CBivariate}): nested univariate polynomials with dedicated operations, used heavily in the coding-theory stack.}
        \li{\em{Fields}: concrete finite-field instances (BabyBear, KoalaBear, Goldilocks, and others), Montgomery arithmetic, computable field extensions, and binary-field / additive-NTT infrastructure.}
        \li{\em{Coding theory}: Reed-Solomon encoding, Gao unique decoding, and Guruswami-Sudan list decoding, with proved correctness.}
    }
}

\p{
    Phase 1 of the roadmap—the theoretical foundation—is essentially complete: core algebraic structure, API coverage, and Mathlib bridges are in place.
    The current focus is Phase 2, performance and efficiency: faster field arithmetic, FFT/NTT-based multiplication, and better evaluation/interpolation pathways, without giving up proof-first correctness.
    See the [roadmap](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/CompPoly/blob/main/ROADMAP.md) for the live status.
}

\p{
    My own role has shifted over [Q1 2026](dhsorens-0026) from doing a large share of the Phase 1 formalization toward coordinating a much larger contributor base for Phase 2—style, CI, contribution standards, and making sure the pieces fit together.
    I also gave a [talk on CompPoly](dhsorens-0028) at ZKProofs 8.
}

